      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "Symlinking doesn't seem to always work per folder basis.\n\nI ended up having the folder in entirely different place for convenience\n\n```\n\nsudo mount --bind '/ssd/LWShared/VRChat/VRChat' '/home/${USER}/.local/share/Steam/steamapps/compatdata/438100/pfx/drive_c/users/steamuser/AppData/LocalLow/VRChat/VRChat'\n\n```\n\nThen mount the Linux .local folder to  LWShared/VRChat\n\n```\n\nsudo mount --bind '/home/${USER}/.local/share/VRChat/VRChat/Avatars/' '/ssd/LWShared/VRChat/VRChat/Avatars/'\n\n```\n\nJust make sure to replace ${USER} as these commands require absolute path with no variables. These mounts will reset upon reboot so you have to make them persistent with systemctl or similar",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2024-06-18T01:02:04.232Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "deletedBy": null,
  "details": "(Edited since I already figured out path to test avatars in comments.)\n\n \n\nCurrently Unity VRCSDK saves test avatars to \"~/.local/share/VRChat/VRChat/Avatars/\"\n\nbut VRChat ran via Proton uses test avatar folder \"~/.local/share/Steam/steamapps/compatdata/438100/pfx/drive_c/users/steamuser/AppData/LocalLow/VRChat/VRChat/\"   \n\n \n\nIt'd be nice to be able to change location where VRCSDK stores the test avatars.\n\nAdditionally it was rather difficult to find out where these test avatars were built. \n\nIn file \"VRCSdkControlPanelAvatarBuilder.cs\", in function \"BuildSuccess\", printing string \"path\"  to console log would make this much clearer.",
